# How the numbers are made

This note covers data sources, tools, the tagging model and its measured accuracy, the statistics,
and what the numbers can and cannot tell you. Every figure below is filled in from the data at
build time (Play Store reviews to {{ a.data_through }}, built {{ a.generated_at }}).

## 1. Data

| Source | What | How |
|---|---|---|
| Google Play reviews, `{{ a.app.id }}` | {{ a.counts.reviews | num }} written reviews, {{ a.counts.first_review }} – {{ a.data_through }} (India store, English) | `google-play-scraper`, newest first, full history on the first run, then incremental |
| Google Play listing | Rating, rating count, star counts, installs, version | Same library; one snapshot per refresh, so a rating history builds up over time ({{ m.history | length }} snapshots so far) |
| Comparison apps | {{ a.listing.benchmarks | map(attribute="name") | join(", ") }}: listing plus the last {{ W.year.days }} days of reviews and replies | Same |
| Other MoveInSync apps | {{ a.listing.siblings | map(attribute="name") | join(", ") }}: listing only | Same |

Reviewer names and avatars are never stored. Timestamps are stored in UTC and reported in India
time.

Google publishes star counts that are rescaled to match its recency-weighted rating (see
`SOURCES.md`). Figures derived from those counts, such as the silent-rater estimate and the
rating scenarios, are therefore approximate. They are labelled that way wherever they appear.

## 2. Issue taxonomy

`taxonomy.yaml` defines {{ tax.issues | length }} codes: {{ tax.specific_codes | length }} specific issues and {{ (tax.issues | length) - (tax.specific_codes | length) }} general ones. They were built by
reading a sample of recent 1–3★ reviews and grouping complaints along the employee journey
described in the brief: {{ journey | join(" → ") }}.

