Because owner and type come from the taxonomy, not from the model, they are consistent everywhere.

## 3. Tagging

- **Rules first.** Short, clearly positive reviews ("good", "nice app") are tagged
  `generic_positive` by a rule: {{ a.counts.rule_tagged | num }} of the {{ a.counts.tag_window | num }} reviews since {{ P.tag_since }}
  ({{ (a.counts.rule_tagged / ([a.counts.tag_window, 1] | max)) | pct }}) never reach a model.
- **Model.** Each review gets 1–3 issue codes plus: who the complaint is about (app / service),
  whether a recent update is blamed, whether safety is raised, the employer if named, and a
  12-word specific. Reviews go {{ P.batch_size }} to a request, at temperature 0, and every answer is validated
  against the taxonomy.
- **Resilience.**
  - Malformed answers are retried, and a batch that keeps failing is split in half.
  - If the primary model fails or times out, a fallback model tags only the gaps.
  - Raw answers are cached per model and prompt version, so an interrupted run loses nothing.
  - Unchanged reviews are never re-tagged.

### Choosing the model

Free NVIDIA NIM models were probed first. Several listed models were not served (404), timed out,
or returned invalid JSON; the ones that answered reliably were the Nemotron-3 family. Those were
scored on the labelled reference sets, and "thinking" was switched off (`@nothink`): with it on, a
request spent its whole token budget reasoning and returned no answer.

